  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🐲 Dragonborn Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Clan name first, personal name second: dragonborn names built the way D&D describes them."
    },
    "intro": "Dragonborn put their clan before themselves, and their names show it. In D&D a dragonborn has a personal name given at birth, but puts the clan name first as a mark of honour, so a dragonborn introduces themselves by family before self. This **dragonborn name generator** builds full names in that order, or gives you a personal name, a clan name or a childhood nickname on its own.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How dragonborn names work",
        "content": "<p>The fifth-edition Player's Handbook (2014) gives dragonborn three kinds of name:</p><ul><li><strong>A clan name</strong>, said first. Dragonborn society is built around clans, and a dragonborn's standing depends on the clan's honour, so putting it first is a statement of loyalty.</li><li><strong>A personal name</strong>, given at birth.</li><li><strong>A childhood name</strong>, a nickname used among clutchmates, either describing them or said with affection. Some stick for life.</li></ul><p>So where a human would say \"Aldric Thorne\", a dragonborn says the equivalent of \"Thorne Aldric\". The generator's full names follow that order: <em>Morrikathul Kerrak</em> is Kerrak of clan Morrikathul.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What the names sound like",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Clan names are long</strong>, often four or five syllables, with rolling and hissing sounds: <em>-thul</em>, <em>-andor</em>, <em>-iax</em>, <em>-esk</em>. They sound old, because they are.</li><li><strong>Personal names are short and hard</strong>, usually two syllables built on <em>rr</em>, <em>zh</em>, <em>kh</em> and <em>sh</em>: Rhazkar, Torvash, Jhessa.</li><li><strong>Female names</strong> tend to end in a vowel (Kalira, Rhiza, Uzhara); <strong>male names</strong> in a consonant (Ghazrin, Zorvash).</li><li><strong>Childhood names are plain words</strong>, often in Common: Quickclaw, Bookworm, Runt. They describe a habit or a moment from the egg-clutch years.</li></ul><p>All the names here are new, built from those patterns, rather than copied from the Player's Handbook.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Using the clan in your story",
        "content": "<p>The clan name is the most useful part of a dragonborn's name for roleplay. A dragonborn who has dishonoured their clan, or been cast out of it, has a decision to make every time they introduce themselves: say the clan name, hide it or drop it.</p><ul><li><strong>Proud:</strong> uses the full name every time, and expects others to know the clan.</li><li><strong>Exiled:</strong> goes by the personal name or childhood name alone, and changes the subject when asked.</li><li><strong>Clanless:</strong> some dragonborn, raised away from other dragonborn, never learned a clan name at all. Use \"Personal name only\" and let the party wonder.</li></ul><p>Draconic ancestry (red, gold, silver and so on) doesn't change how the name is built, but it's a good guide to sound: a red-blooded clan might favour harsher names than a silver one.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Why do dragonborn say their clan name first?",
        "answer": "In D&D's Player's Handbook, dragonborn put their clan name before their personal name as a mark of honour, because a dragonborn's place in the world depends on their clan."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a dragonborn childhood name?",
        "answer": "A nickname used among clutchmates, the dragonborn they grew up with. It's usually a plain descriptive word, like Quickclaw or Bookworm, and some dragonborn keep it as adults."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are these names from the Player's Handbook?",
        "answer": "No. They're new names in the same style, so your dragonborn won't share a name with an example from the book."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these in Baldur's Gate 3?",
        "answer": "Yes. <a href=\"baldurs-gate-3-name-generator.html\">BG3</a> uses fifth-edition dragonborn, and you can type any name in the character creator. Clan-first full names can be long, so a personal name alone may fit the name box better."
      }
    ]
  }
}
